# The spec, against its own budget and against the compiler.
#
# Ports the two Rust modules that are TESTS and therefore die with the
# bootstrap: `archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/src/measure/gate.rs` (the §1.6 budget's gate) and
# `archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/src/measure/spec.rs` (the spec-versus-compiler checks).
# Panel 085's spec-warden vetoed the archive over exactly this — §1.6's
# ceiling had one live enforcer, and it was `cfg(test)` inside a crate about
# to be archived.
#
# WHY THE SPEC IS WORTH SEVEN CHECKS. It is not documentation: §1.6 makes it
# **the prompt** — the whole context a model writing Heroes ever has. So a
# rule the compiler enforces and the spec omits is not terseness, it is a
# wrong briefing, and this project shipped one for four milestones: panel 013
# ratified `(function(A) -> B)` while the spec still said `(fn(A) -> B)`,
# which is a *reserved-word error in every position*.
#
# THE CHECKS
#   budget      the spec measures what the record says, and so does the
#               reserved-word registry — the number moves only in a commit
#               that says why;
#   ledger      the record of every amendment agrees with the pin, three ways:
#               its row count, its newest figure, and today's measurement
#               (panel 086 R2 — the check the ledger never had);
#   headroom    the ceiling still has room for what is mortgaged against it;
#   names       every name the compiler reserves appears in the spec, because
#               a name a model cannot see is a name it will redeclare;
#   offered     every name the spec offers exists, because a name a model
#               reads and the compiler lacks is a call to nothing;
#   rejected    no word the lexer refuses appears in the spec's code;
#   inventory   the spec's characters above ASCII are punctuation, which is
#               the premise `selfhost/measure/pieces.hero` is written on;
#   colouring   the editor's grammar admits the escapes the compiler accepts
#               and calls the words it calls keywords, because that file is a
#               copy of the language that reads from nothing (added 2026-09-13,
#               after it painted a correct program as an error for three days);
#               and since 2026-10-02 it paints an `f` literal's two brace
#               escapes and its lone brace as the lexer reads them (`braces`);
#               and since 2026-10-07 every escape § 2 names, and each other
#               spelling of its escape by code, is painted as one exactly
#               where the lexer reads one, in each of the three literals, the
#               grammar's rules RUN by `textmate.hero` (`escapes_painted`,
#               defect 377).
#
# WHERE THE COMPILER'S SIDE COMES FROM. The two lists — what it reserves and
# what it refuses — are read out of `selfhost/`'s own source, not copied
# here: `builtins.hero`'s table and `keywords.hero`'s match arms. A copy
# would be a second place for the truth to live, and both extractions carry
# a FLOOR, because an extractor that silently reads nothing turns two checks
# into decorations.

use report
use floors
use shell
use spec_text
use strings
use textmate

constant SPEC: str
    "spec/heroes-spec.md"

constant REGISTRY: str
    "spec/reserved-words.md"

# The ledger — one row per spec amendment, with what it cost and what paid for
# it. It lived in `archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/src/measure/gate.rs`'s doc comment until panel
# 086 moved it here, verbatim, ahead of the archive: `docs/measurements/` is the
# one candidate directory that is both on CLAUDE.md §14's never-rewritten list
# and already outside `suite_records.hero`'s walk.
constant LEDGER: str
    "docs/measurements/010-spec-budget-ledger.md"

# One row per spec amendment. Pinned for the reason the alias table's is — it is
# the tripwire that makes a SILENTLY DROPPED row fail, which comparing the newest
# row alone would not.
#
# **The count is not restated in this comment, since 2026-09-12.** It said
# *"Measured 2026-08-24: 39 rows (the place store's row)"* while the constant
# below read 69: the number moved thirty times and the sentence above it moved
# never, so a reader met a date, a figure, and a witness that were all true of a
# document thirty amendments ago. Nothing could catch it — design.md §1.6's own
# rule is that a number may live in a second place only if it is CHECKED or
# DATED, and prose beside the constant is neither, however dated it looks. The
# constant is the count; `ledger` below is what holds it to the file.
constant LEDGER_ROWS: i64
    102

# design.md §1.6's figure, pinned — the number `heroes measure` computes from the
# spec, restated here because something re-derives and compares it on every run.
# The HISTORY is the ledger above (`docs/measurements/010`), and `ledger` below is
# what keeps the two from disagreeing.
#
# **Measured 2026-08-18, by both compilers, on this spec.** A spec amendment
# turns this red, and the only way to green is to write the new number into
# the commit that made it true — which is precisely the moment panel 012's
# rule asks for a named removal or a registered falsifiable prediction. The
# check does not enforce that rule: it makes its question unavoidable, and
# the answer lands in the commit body where a reader can audit it.
constant SPEC_TOKENS: i64
    7513

# The registry's own size, gated separately — it is NOT part of §1.6's budget
# (panel 035 D: the spec is *the prompt*, and compiler output can never be in
# one). It gets a number anyway, because it grew +85 across eight milestones
# with no commit ever naming a delta, for the plain reason that `measure`
# defaults to the other file.
constant REGISTRY_TOKENS: i64
    916

# **The ceiling, 10240 by author decision 2026-09-14**, taken while
# M-marked-acquisition was landing `acquires <releaser>` and the document stood
# at 7921 real against 8192 — 211 free once the FFI floor is deducted, which is
# two forms' room and not three. **It relaxes nothing**: §1.6's payment rule is
# unconditional at every level, with no threshold to be under, so every row of
# `docs/measurements/010` is owed exactly as before.
#
# **It is 10 × 1024 and not 10000**, because `site/src/lib/claims.ts`'s
# `ceilingK` refuses a ceiling that is not a whole number of K and the pages say
# it in K — the site derives this constant rather than restating it, so a
# number that is not a multiple of 1024 stops the site's build by design.
#
# It was 8192 by author decision 2026-09-12, which made room inside the
# spec for the grammar's productions (panel 133). It relaxes nothing: §1.6's
# payment rule is unconditional at every level, with no threshold to be under.
#
# It was 6144 by author decision 2026-09-09, and that is where it started
# carrying its instrument (panel 123 R3): `claude-opus-5` through
# `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`, where the spec measured 5094 on the day it
# was set. Panel 024's 4096 was the maximum over two VENDORED tokenisers and
# the larger of those is OpenAI's, so the document was 998 over a ceiling it
# was told it was 40 under. `SPEC_TOKENS` above stays the VENDORED figure
# deliberately: it answers "did the spec move and nobody say so", which it
# always answered correctly, and re-basing it would make the next ledger row
# incomparable with the first thirty-eight.
constant CEILING: i64
    10240

# THE RECORD: the reader's own count, and the same four constants
# `selfhost/cli/measure.hero` holds — for the reason `CEILING` is in two
# places, one printed and one checked, with `recorded` below as what stops
# them drifting (defect 017's class). **This is the number the ceiling is
# judged against**, and `SPEC_TOKENS` above is not: that one is vendored and
# answers a different question, *did the spec move and nobody say so*, which
# it always answered correctly. Refreshed by hand, by
# `heroes measure spec/heroes-spec.md --refresh`, never by a check: panel 123
# R1 refused a key-gated gate, because the repository is public and GitHub
# passes no secret to a fork's pull request, so such a check is structurally
# red on every fork PR forever.
constant REAL_TOKENS: i64
    9887

constant REAL_MODEL: str
    "claude-opus-5"

constant REAL_TAKEN: str
    "2026-10-10"

# The contract's own record, pinned here the way the spec's is, and cross-checked
# against `selfhost/measure/pinned.hero` by `recorded` below. **It carries a
# vendored count where the spec carries a digest**, and the reason is measured:
# `CLAUDE.md` takes about 34 commits a week against the spec's 17, so a digest
# would withhold the verdict about that often, clearable only by a network
# refresh and not clearable at all on a fork PR (panel 134, the compiler seat's
# veto). `CONTRACT_TOKENS` is keyless, offline, and does three jobs at once: the
# staleness detector, the delta gate's previous level, and the movement detector
# the contract never had.
constant CONTRACT_REAL_TOKENS: i64
    8167

# **The contract's date is its own, and this constant is why.** `REAL_TAKEN`
# above judged BOTH documents until 2026-09-13, and it worked for exactly as
# long as the two were refreshed on the same day. The moment panel 139's § 3
# amendment landed, the spec's count was re-taken and the contract's was not —
# and `real` went red saying `measure CLAUDE.md` fails to print a date the
# contract had never claimed. `selfhost/measure/pinned.hero` has carried
# `SPEC_REAL_TAKEN` and `CONTRACT_REAL_TAKEN` separately all along, so this is
# the harness catching up with the compiler's own record rather than a check
# being loosened: two independent documents, two independent refreshes, two
# dates. A shared constant between them was a coupling nothing had stated.
constant CONTRACT_REAL_TAKEN: str
    "2026-09-29"

constant CONTRACT_TOKENS: i64
    6145

# The width of one commit's allowed growth, in VENDORED tokens, on both judged
# documents. §1.6 queued this figure and panel 134 landed it; the reasoning and
# what it does NOT buy are on `DELTA_GATE` in `selfhost/cli/measure.hero`, which
# `recorded` holds this to.
constant DELTA_GATE: i64
    50

# The spec this count is OF, content-addressed, so a stale number is loud
# rather than reassuring. `selfhost/cli/digest.hero` computes it and
# `measure` refuses to give a verdict when it disagrees.
constant SPEC_DIGEST: str
    "4c6a7219665fd821"

# The operating contract, weighed on the same scale as the language.
#
# **Why it is here and not in `suite_records.hero`.** That suite gets no compiler
# and this number comes from `heroes measure`, which is the same INSTRUMENT that
# decides the spec's budget above: `claude-opus-5` through
# `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`, pinned and refreshed by hand.
#
# **One instrument, two jobs — and the sentence here used to claim one job**
# (author, ratifying panel 134). It read *"two documents with ceilings belong on
# one scale"*, which compared quantities that are not comparable. `CEILING` above
# bears on the LANGUAGE: every token is a feature paying rent, and squeezing is
# the point. This one is HOUSEKEEPING, and when it is reached no rule is cut —
# one MOVES, into `.claude/rules/` where it loads only when its paths are
# touched, into the case law, or into a skill. So this number measures the fixed
# cost every session pays on every turn, and nothing about the language.
#
# **What it is for, measured 2026-09-07.** `CLAUDE.md` was **1247 lines and 22689
# tokens**, against 3871 for the whole language, having grown 118 -> 555 -> 1139
# -> 1247 lines in 35 days over 90 commits: a paragraph per incident, each one a
# correct rule in the shape of a diary. **It is 341 lines now** — this line said
# 322 until 2026-09-12, which is the shape of defect this file exists to catch,
# in this file — with the stories moved to `docs/records/contract/case-law.md` and
# the tree-local rules to `.claude/rules/`.
#
# **WHAT THE COST OF A LONG CONTRACT IS, corrected 2026-09-12.** This comment
# said *"Claude Code's own documentation asks for under 200 lines and warns that
# a longer file reduces adherence"*. Panel 134's historian fetched that page and
# **the figure is not on it**: *"under 200 lines"* is third-party blog content
# this repository had been citing as a vendor rule. What the page does say,
# verbatim, is that *"bloated CLAUDE.md files cause Claude to ignore your actual
# instructions"* — a mechanism, not a threshold — and the measured evidence
# nearby is of the adjacent effect rather than this one: reliability falling as
# input grows (Chroma's context-rot study across eighteen models, Liu et al.'s
# lost-in-the-middle). **There is no published size at which adherence falls.**
# So the ceiling below is an informed CHOICE and says so, instead of implying a
# threshold somebody measured.
#
# **A CEILING and not a pinned equality, which is the opposite of `SPEC_TOKENS`
# above, deliberately.** The spec is budget-governed to the token, so any delta
# there must be stated in the commit that makes it. The contract is amended by
# author instruction and often by a sentence, so an exact pin would go red on a
# typo and be raised mechanically until nobody believed it, which is the failure
# mode of a number with no argument behind it. What this holds is the disease
# rather than the byte: unbounded regrowth.
#
# **12288 = 7306 measured + 4982 of room** (author, 2026-09-12). It is set HIGH
# on purpose, because reaching it should mean the tidying is genuinely due rather
# than firing at every amendment and being raised on reflex. The room buys about
# fifty days at the +97 real tokens a day the contract grew over the five days to
# 2026-09-12, and about 1.7 times the current document; 16384 was refused as
# letting the per-turn cost nearly double, 10240 as thirty days.
#
# **And the LEVEL is only half of it.** A ceiling that rings about twice a year
# does no daily work, and panel 134's historian found no internally-owned,
# raisable size budget that ever held its level — the one shape that held is a
# ratchet, where the human is out of the raise. What watches the movement is
# `DELTA_GATE` below.
constant CONTRACT: str
    "CLAUDE.md"

constant CONTRACT_CEILING: i64
    12288

# The floor for file I/O, `args()` and `exit(code)` — panel 030 R3, which
# refuted "they are plain externs" by compiling it. The floor belongs to the
# FFI rather than to the milestone that pays it, so the name says so.
constant FFI_FLOOR: i64
    60

constant BUILTIN_SOURCE: str
    "selfhost/inventory.hero"

constant KEYWORD_SOURCE: str
    "selfhost/keywords.hero"

# The editor's grammar, and the table it must agree with.
#
# **This file is a FOURTH copy of what the language is** — after the compiler,
# the spec and the site — and it is the only one that is a copy at all.
# `site/src/lib/tables.ts` reads its keywords out of `selfhost/keywords.hero`
# and its types out of § 3's table, each behind a floor, and
# `site/src/lib/highlight.ts` does not colour escapes at all. The grammar is the
# one place the language is written down a second time by hand and read from
# nothing, and on 2026-09-13 that cost what this file's own doc comment
# predicts: it admitted **four** escapes where the spec gives **five**, so a
# legal `\r` fell through to the rule below it and a CORRECT program was painted
# `invalid.illegal` in the editor. `print("a\rb")` runs at exit 0.
#
# The defect had been **measured three days earlier** and written into
# `.claude/rules/diagnostics-and-goldens.md`, which named the reason it could
# survive in the same paragraph: *"Nothing judged either file until then, which
# is exactly how the grammar rotted in silence."* A second hand-correction with
# no instrument would rot the same way. This is the instrument.
constant GRAMMAR: str
    "editors/vscode/syntaxes/heroes.tmLanguage.json"

constant ESCAPE_SOURCE: str
    "selfhost/escape.hero"

# Measured 2026-09-14: `escape_text` has **six** arms — `n` `t` `r` `\` `"` `'`
# — and `keyword()` has 21. The floor said 3 for one day, because only the
# letters were compared; panel 146's review showed that leaving the other three
# out let a grammar drop them, or SWAP the two quotes between the string class
# and the character class, with this check at 20 passed and correct programs
# painted `invalid.illegal`.
constant LEAST_ESCAPES: i64
    6

constant LEAST_KEYWORDS: i64
    21

constant LOWER: str
    "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"

# Measured 2026-08-18: 37 rows in the table, 23 words across the match arms.
constant LEAST_BUILTINS: i64
    37

constant LEAST_FOREIGN: i64
    23

# Every character above ASCII the spec holds, sorted — the falsifier for the
# pre-tokeniser's ASCII reading of "letter" and "digit". The SET is pinned
# and the count is not: another em dash is not news, and a letter is.
constant ABOVE_ASCII: [str]
    [
        "·"
        "—"
        "…"
        "→"
    ]

# The thirteen sections, by number and name, in order: the shape
# `.claude/rules/spec-shape.md` names, pinned here so a heading moved, renamed
# or renumbered is a red check and not a reader's impression. A number, once
# assigned, never changes, and a new section goes at the end (CL-069: the
# contract's `§1`–`§15` survived a rewrite for exactly this reason).
constant SECTIONS: [str]
    [
        "Files and layout"
        "Literals"
        "Types"
        "Top-level declarations"
        "Bindings"
        "Failure: `T?`"
        "Operators"
        "Control flow"
        "Functions and calls"
        "Strings, arrays, maps"
        "Built-ins"
        "Tests and holes"
        "FFI"
    ]

function run_all(scratch: str, compiler: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    text = read_file(SPEC)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec", detail: "  cannot read " + SPEC)
        return ok(r)
    budget(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler)
    contract(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, ceiling: CONTRACT_CEILING)
    spendable(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler)
    recorded(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler)
    ledger(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler)
    reserved = names_in_table(path: BUILTIN_SOURCE, marker: "Builtin(name: ")
    refused = words_in_arms(path: KEYWORD_SOURCE, marker: "=> ok(ForeignWord(")
    named(@r, spec: text.must(), reserved: reserved)
    offered(@r, spec: text.must(), reserved: reserved)
    rejected(@r, spec: text.must(), refused: refused)
    inventory(@r, spec: text.must())
    shape(@r, spec: text.must())
    anchors(@r)
    colouring(@r)
    braces(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler)
    escapes_painted(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, spec: text.must())
    return ok(r)

# The two gated numbers, and the headroom.
function budget(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str)
    measured = maximum_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: SPEC)

    if measured.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/budget", detail: "  cannot measure " + SPEC + " — " + why(measured))
    else if measured.must() != SPEC_TOKENS
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/budget",
            detail: "  the spec measures " + measured.must().to_str() + " and the record says " + SPEC_TOKENS.to_str() + " — a delta of " + (measured.must() - SPEC_TOKENS).to_str() + ".\n  If the change is intended, write the new number into SPEC_TOKENS in the SAME\n  commit, and put the delta plus its payment in the commit body: either a named\n  removal, or a registered falsifiable prediction naming BOTH the instrument that\n  will score it and the milestone it is scored at — and the instrument has to\n  exist today (panel 012, as amended by panel 046). A prediction naming an\n  instrument nobody has built is an observation, not a payment.\n  If it is not intended, the spec grew by accident, which is the case this\n  check exists for."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)
    registry = maximum_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: REGISTRY)

    if registry.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/registry", detail: "  cannot measure " + REGISTRY)
    else if registry.must() != REGISTRY_TOKENS
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/registry",
            detail: "  the registry measures " + registry.must().to_str() + " and the record says " + REGISTRY_TOKENS.to_str() + " — write the new number down. It is not in §1.6's budget, and it grew +85 across eight milestones with nobody naming a delta."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

    # The headroom, which is the one thing §1.6 says must survive every
    # raise: it is real, not notional.
    #
    # **REAL_TOKENS and not SPEC_TOKENS, since 2026-09-09.** This is the one
    # question the vendored figure was never able to answer: it read 3995
    # against 4096 and reported 40 free while the reader's own tokeniser read
    # 5094, so the document was 998 over a line it was told it was under
    # (docs/measurements/023). What a ceiling judges is what the READER pays.
    if REAL_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR >= CEILING
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/headroom",
            detail: "  the reader's count plus the FFI floor is " + (REAL_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR).to_str() + " against the " + CEILING.to_str() + " ceiling — what is mortgaged no longer fits"
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The operating contract against its ceiling, on the same scale as the spec.
#
# **The ceiling is a parameter and not read from the constant here**, so a test
# can fire this check with a stub compiler and a ceiling of its own. §9 asks
# every verifier check for a test that makes it fail, and a check that can only
# be exercised by making the real contract enormous has no such test.
function contract(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str, ceiling: i64)
    measured = real_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: CONTRACT)

    if measured.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/contract", detail: "  cannot measure " + CONTRACT + " — " + why(measured))
    else if measured.must() > ceiling
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/contract",
            detail: "  " + CONTRACT + " measures " + measured.must().to_str() + " tokens against a ceiling of " + ceiling.to_str() + " — over by " + (measured.must() - ceiling).to_str() + ".\n  Every session loads all of it on every turn, the five panel seats included,\n  and a longer contract is one whose rules are followed less: that is what the\n  1247-line, 22689-token file of 2026-09-07 cost, against 3871 for the whole\n  language.\n  The repair is not a bigger number, and it is not cutting a rule either. MOVE\n  one: into .claude/rules/, where it loads only when its paths are touched, or\n  into docs/records/contract/case-law.md as a story the rule then cites, or into a\n  skill. Raising CONTRACT_CEILING is an author decision and goes in the commit\n  body with its reason."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# THE LOCK (panel 086 R2). Three numbers must agree: the record's row count and
# `LEDGER_ROWS`, the newest row's figure and `SPEC_TOKENS`, and `SPEC_TOKENS` and
# what `heroes measure` reports today.
#
# **This check is what the ledger has never had.** Its only coupling to the
# enforced constant was ADJACENCY — the newest row sat two lines above
# `pub const SPEC_TOKENS` — and adjacency is a habit, not a mechanism. Two seats
# of panel 086 named the same hole independently, and the warden supplied the base
# rate for what habits do here: `REGISTRY_TOKENS` drifted +85 across eight
# milestones with no commit naming a delta, and one ledger row stood false for
# 6h46m. The rule the sitting adopted: a number may live in a second place only if
# it is checked or dated — a row is dated, a pin is checked, and this is what
# checks the pin against the row.
function ledger(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str)
    text = read_file(LEDGER)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/ledger",
            detail: "  cannot read " + LEDGER + " — the ledger is the record of every spec amendment, and panel 086 R1 put it there"
        )
        return
    figures = ledger_figures(text.must())

    if figures.len() != LEDGER_ROWS
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/ledger",
            detail: "  " + LEDGER + " has " + figures.len().to_str() + " rows and the record says " + LEDGER_ROWS.to_str() + " — a row that vanishes takes an amendment's payment with it"
        )
        return
    newest = figures[figures.len() - 1]

    if newest != SPEC_TOKENS
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/ledger",
            detail: "  the newest ledger row says " + newest.to_str() + " and SPEC_TOKENS says " + SPEC_TOKENS.to_str() + ".\n  These are the same number in two places, one dated and one checked: an\n  amendment moved one of them and not the other. Fix the one that is wrong in\n  the commit that made it wrong — " + LEDGER + " and this file."
        )
        return
    measured = maximum_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: SPEC)

    if measured.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/ledger", detail: "  cannot measure " + SPEC + " — " + why(measured))
        return

    if measured.must() != newest
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/ledger",
            detail: "  the ledger's newest row says " + newest.to_str() + " and the compiler measures " + measured.must().to_str() + " — the record and the document disagree"
        )
        return
    report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every ledger row's figure, in file order — the newest last.
#
# A row is a line opening `| ` whose FIRST CELL IS A NUMBER, which is a fact about
# the line in hand rather than a premise about the table's shape: the header row
# (`| value | when | …`) and the `|---|` separator are excluded because their
# first cell is not a number, not because they are counted and subtracted.
function ledger_figures(text: str) -> [i64]
    found: [i64] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text)
        if !strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "| ")
            continue
        rest = strings.without_prefix(text: row, prefix: "| ")
        at = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: " |")

        if at.is_err()
            continue
        value = strings.to_number(strings.trimmed(rest.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())))

        if !value.is_err()
            found @ found.push(value.must())

    return found

# Why a measurement could not be taken — the compiler's own words, which is
# what tells a reader whether the tables are missing or the tool is.
function why(got: i64?) -> str
    return match got
        .ok _  => ""
        .err e => e.msg

# `heroes measure <path>`'s vendored maximum. Exit 1 is a BREACH rather than a
# failure to run, so the number is read either way and the verdict is this
# suite's own.
#
# **IT KEYS ON THE ROW'S NAME AND NOT ON ITS PROSE, since 2026-09-09.** It used
# to look for the words *"the binding number"*, and panel 123 R6 renamed that
# label to *"a lower bound, not the reader's tokeniser"* — because the vendored
# maximum is not the reader's number and calling it binding is what cost 998
# tokens. The rename turned this suite red with `measure printed no binding
# number`, which is the right failure for the wrong reason: a check that reads a
# human-facing label breaks when the label is corrected. `maximum` is the row's
# identity and prose is not.
function maximum_of(scratch: str, compiler: str, path: str) -> i64?
    said = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "measure", path])?

    if said.code != 0 && said.code != 1
        return fail(code: "cannot_measure", msg: "`measure` exited " + said.code.to_str() + ": " + said.err)

    for row in strings.lines(said.out)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "  maximum")
            words = strings.words(row)

            if words.len() >= 2
                return strings.to_number(words[1])

    return fail(code: "cannot_measure", msg: "`measure` printed no `maximum` row")

# The REAL row, and this function is the whole of panel 134's R5.
#
# **The defect it closes would have been silent and permanent.** `maximum_of`
# above accepts exit 0 AND exit 1, and `measure` prints the `maximum` row before
# it can withhold a verdict — so a `contract` check reading `maximum` goes on
# comparing a VENDORED number to a ceiling that is now a `claude-opus-5` REAL
# one, and passes forever on the scale this repair exists to abolish. The gap it
# would have hidden is not small: 5491 against 7306, which is the 1806 the
# contract was already over when nobody could see it.
#
# **It refuses exit 1 where `maximum_of` allows it**, and that is the second
# half. Exit 1 from `measure` means the verdict was WITHHELD — a stale digest, or
# a vendored drift past the window — and a withheld verdict has no real row to
# read. Taking the number anyway would be reading a count of a document that is
# no longer the one on disk.
function real_of(scratch: str, compiler: str, path: str) -> i64?
    said = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "measure", path])?

    if said.code != 0
        return fail(
            code: "cannot_measure",
            msg: "`measure " + path + "` exited " + said.code.to_str() + ", so no verdict was given and there is no binding number to read: " + said.out + said.err
        )

    for row in strings.lines(said.out)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "  real")
            words = strings.words(row)

            if words.len() >= 2
                return strings.to_number(words[1])

    return fail(
        code: "cannot_measure",
        msg: "`measure " + path + "` printed no `real` row — the ceiling is a `claude-opus-5` count and the vendored maximum is not it (panel 134)"
    )

# THE TWO INSTRUMENTS AGREE ON WHAT IS SPENDABLE, which is defect 017's
# repair (2026-09-08, found by panel 118's spec-warden about its own
# instrument). `heroes measure` printed `CEILING - highest` and knew
# nothing of `FFI_FLOOR`, so the number a session reads before amending the
# spec was 60 larger than the number this file goes red at, and the looser
# of the two is the one reached for first. `measure` now states the SUM
# this check compares rather than a subtraction of its own — a "usable
# room" figure is off by one the moment `>=` is read as `>`, which it was,
# in that repair's own first version — and this asks that the sum it prints
# is the sum computed here.
function spendable(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str)
    said = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "measure", SPEC])

    # The refusal's own words, here and below (defect 317).
    if said.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/spendable", detail: "  `measure " + SPEC + "`: " + shell.refusal(said))
        return
    want = "is " + (REAL_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR).to_str() + " and the check goes red at " + CEILING.to_str()

    if !strings.contains(text: said.must().out, needle: want)
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/spendable",
            detail: "  `measure " + SPEC + "` does not say \"" + want + "\".\n  The two instruments have drifted: this file goes red at REAL_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR\n  >= CEILING, and what `measure` prints is what a session reads BEFORE amending\n  the spec. Whichever of the two moved, the other has to move with it — the\n  number is one number in two places, one printed and one checked (defect 017)."
        )
        return

    # The same question of the contract, whose ceiling ALSO lives in two
    # places now: `measure` shouted BREACH at `CLAUDE.md` for every token
    # above 4096 while this file held it to CONTRACT_CEILING, so the tool
    # invited the one repair that is always wrong here — cutting a rule to
    # make a number smaller. Defect 017's class, second witness.
    told = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "measure", CONTRACT])

    if told.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/spendable", detail: "  `measure " + CONTRACT + "`: " + shell.refusal(told))
        return
    owns = "against the contract's own " + CONTRACT_CEILING.to_str()

    if !strings.contains(text: told.must().out, needle: owns)
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/spendable",
            detail: "  `measure " + CONTRACT + "` does not say \"" + owns + "\".\n  The contract is weighed on this scale and not against §1.6's 4096, and the\n  number is one number in two places (defect 017)."
        )
        return
    report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every name the compiler reserves appears in the spec.
function named(@r: report.Report, spec: str, reserved: [str])
    if !floors.sound(@r, label: "spec/names", found: reserved.len(), floor: LEAST_BUILTINS, what: "built-ins read out of " + BUILTIN_SOURCE)
        return
    mentioned: {str: bool} @ {}

    for word in spec_text.identifiers_in_code(spec)
        mentioned[word] @ true

    missing: [str] @ []

    for name in reserved
        if mentioned[name].is_err()
            missing @ missing.push(name)

    if missing.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/names",
            detail: "  the compiler reserves these and the spec never mentions them: " + missing.join(" ") + "\n  A name a model cannot see is a name it will redeclare."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# Every name the spec offers exists in the compiler.
function offered(@r: report.Report, spec: str, reserved: [str])
    listed = spec_text.offered_builtins(spec)

    if listed.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/offered",
            detail: "  the spec's `Built-ins:` sentence could not be read at all"
        )
        return
    names = listed.must()

    if names.len() < 10
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/offered",
            detail: "  the built-in list reads as " + names.len().to_str() + " names, which is truncated: " + names.join(" ")
        )
        return

    if names[names.len() - 1] != "range"
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/offered",
            detail: "  the built-in list no longer ends at `range` — the sentence delimiter found the wrong full stop, and every name after it is now unchecked: " + names.join(" ")
        )
        return
    known: {str: bool} @ {}

    for name in reserved
        known[name] @ true

    absent: [str] @ []

    for name in names
        if known[name].is_err()
            absent @ absent.push(name)

    if absent.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/offered",
            detail: "  the spec offers these and the compiler has no such built-in: " + absent.join(" ")
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# No word the lexer refuses appears in the spec's code.
function rejected(@r: report.Report, spec: str, refused: [str])
    if !floors.sound(@r, label: "spec/rejected", found: refused.len(), floor: LEAST_FOREIGN, what: "refused words read out of " + KEYWORD_SOURCE)
        return
    mentioned: {str: bool} @ {}

    for word in spec_text.identifiers_in_code(spec)
        mentioned[word] @ true

    found: [str] @ []

    for word in refused
        if !mentioned[word].is_err()
            found @ found.push(word)

    if found.len() > 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/rejected",
            detail: "  these appear in the spec's code and the lexer refuses them with a prescribed fix: " + found.join(" ") + "\n  `fn` is the case that happened: the registry called it an error AND the spec used it in the function type, so every function-typed annotation a model wrote from the spec was a compile error — for four milestones."
        )
    else
        report.keep_pass(@r)

# The spec's characters above ASCII are the four punctuation marks the
# pre-tokeniser was written against.
function inventory(@r: report.Report, spec: str)
    got = spec_text.above_ascii(spec)

    if got == ABOVE_ASCII
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "spec/inventory",
        detail: "  the spec now holds " + got.join(" ") + " above ASCII, and the record says " + ABOVE_ASCII.join(" ") + ".\n  `selfhost/measure/pieces.hero` reads a letter as A-Z a-z and a digit as 0-9\n  where the Rust it ports asks Unicode. Ask of the new character: is it a\n  letter or a digit in any language? If it is, that claim is dead and the\n  token count has quietly drifted. If it is punctuation, add it here."
    )

# The headings are the thirteen `SECTIONS` names, numbered from 1, in order,
# and nothing else opens with `## `.
function shape(@r: report.Report, spec: str)
    found: [str] @ []
    in_fence: bool @ false

    for line in strings.lines(spec)
        # A `## ` inside a fence is a Heroes section heading (spec § 1), not
        # one of the document's; panel 126's compiler seat showed the hole.
        if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "```")
            in_fence @ !in_fence

        if !in_fence && strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "## ")
            found @ found.push(strings.without_prefix(text: line, prefix: "## "))

    if found == numbered_sections()
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "spec/shape",
        detail: "  the spec's headings read:\n    " + found.join("\n    ") + "\n  and `.claude/rules/spec-shape.md` names:\n    " + numbered_sections().join("\n    ") + "\n  A number, once assigned, never changes, and a new section goes at the end (CL-069)."
    )

# `1. Files and layout` … `13. FFI`: what the headings read after `## `.
function numbered_sections() -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < SECTIONS.len()
        out @ out.push(to_str(i + 1) + ". " + SECTIONS[i])
        i @ i + 1

    return out

# Every citation of the spec BY SECTION in the compiler's source and the tests
# names a section that exists: `spec § 7` by number, `spec § Operators` by
# title. A citation is judged when the text before its `§` names the spec, so
# `CLAUDE.md § 3` and design.md's `§4.19` are not this check's business; and a
# line number, `spec:187`, is not judged at all, because it cannot be: it is the
# one citation shape no instrument can see (CL-037), which is why
# `.claude/rules/spec-shape.md` asks for the section instead.
function anchors(@r: report.Report)
    offenders: [str] @ []
    other = other_headings()

    for dir in ["selfhost", "tests"]
        files = shell.found_under(dir: dir, suffix: ".hero", reference: "")

        if files.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/anchors", detail: "  cannot walk " + dir)
            return

        for path in files.must()
            # The instrument's own file carries the citation forms as strings
            # and as test cases, and is the one file that cannot be judged by
            # itself.
            if strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: "harness/suite_spec.hero")
                continue
            body = read_file(path)

            if body.is_err()
                continue
            number: i64 @ 1

            for line in strings.lines(body.must())
                for bad in unknown_anchors(line, other: other)
                    offenders @ offenders.push("    " + path + ":" + to_str(number) + " cites § " + bad)

                number @ number + 1

    if offenders.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "spec/anchors",
        detail: "  these cite a section of the spec that does not exist:\n" + offenders.join("\n") + "\n  A citation is `spec § N` or `spec § <title>`, with N and the title from\n  `.claude/rules/spec-shape.md`; a title never changes and a number never moves."
    )

# The section citations in one line that name nothing. A `§ ` is judged when
# the 32 characters before it name the spec as a word (`inspect` and `respect`
# do not, and that was a probe of panel 126's compiler seat), or when they name
# no document at all and a title follows: a bare `(§ Widths)` is a citation of
# nothing, and a bare `(§ Functions and calls)` is judged and holds. A `§ ` in
# a window that names another document, CLAUDE.md, design.md, the ROADMAP, a
# README, the reserved-word registry, is that document's business, and so is
# a `§ ` right after any markdown file's name but the spec's own
# (`names_a_markdown_file`); and a bare `§ Commands` that is one of THEIR
# headings passes through `other`, read from those files once by `anchors`.
function unknown_anchors(line: str, other: [str]) -> [str]
    cs = line.chars()
    out: [str] @ []
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i + 1 < cs.len()
        if cs[i] == "§" && cs[i + 1] == " "
            before = cs.slice(from: window_start(i), to: i).join("")
            rest = cs.slice(from: i + 2, to: cs.len())

            if names_another_document(before) || names_a_markdown_file(before)
                i @ i + 1
                continue

            if mentions_spec(before) || rest.len() > 0 && is_capital(rest[0]) && !opens_with_any(rest: rest, titles: other)
                if !names_a_section(rest)
                    out @ out.push(rest.slice(from: 0, to: shortest(a: rest.len(), b: 24)).join(""))
        i @ i + 1

    return out

# `spec` as a word: at the start of the window or after a character that is
# not a letter, so `spec § 7`, `(spec, § FFI)` and `heroes-spec.md` count and
# `inspect` and `respect` do not.
function mentions_spec(before: str) -> bool
    cs = before.chars()
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i + 4 <= cs.len()
        if cs.slice(from: i, to: i + 4).join("") == "spec"
            if i == 0 || !is_letter(cs[i - 1])
                return true
        i @ i + 1

    return false

function names_another_document(before: str) -> bool
    for name in ["CLAUDE", "design", "reserved-words", "ROADMAP", "README", "DESIGN-LOG", "case-law", "journal", "panel", "measurements", "site/", "DONE", "SCHEDULED", "DECIDE", "DEFECTS", "LEARN", ".env"]
        if strings.contains(text: before, needle: name)
            return true

    return false

# **A `§ ` right after a markdown file's name cites THAT file** (the round of
# 2026-10-02's third gate): `.claude/rules/verification.md` § Bounded discovery
# is the rules file's section, and the list above names no rules file, nor
# could a name in it see one the 32-character window cuts, as it cuts
# `.claude/rules/diagnostics-and-goldens.md`. So the name is read where the
# window ends, backticked or quoted or bare, and asked of its own suffix. The
# spec's own file is the exception: `spec/heroes-spec.md` § 13 is judged as
# `spec § 13` is.
function names_a_markdown_file(before: str) -> bool
    name: str @ strings.trimmed(before)

    while strings.ends_with(text: name, suffix: "`") || strings.ends_with(text: name, suffix: "\"")
        name @ name.slice(from: 0, to: name.len() - 1)

    return strings.ends_with(text: name, suffix: ".md") && !strings.ends_with(text: name, suffix: "heroes-spec.md")

function opens_with_any(rest: [str], titles: [str]) -> bool
    text = rest.join("")

    for title in titles
        if title.len() > 0 && strings.starts_with(text: text, prefix: title)
            return true

    # A title cut by a line wrap, `§ Where we` for `Where we are`, three
    # characters or more, is taken as the title it opens.
    head = strings.trimmed(text)

    if head.len() >= 3
        for title in titles
            if title.len() >= 3 && strings.starts_with(text: title, prefix: head)
                return true

    return false

function is_letter(ch: str) -> bool
    if ch.len() != 1
        return false
    return ch[0] >= 65 && ch[0] <= 90 || ch[0] >= 97 && ch[0] <= 122

function is_capital(ch: str) -> bool
    if ch.len() != 1
        return false
    return ch[0] >= 65 && ch[0] <= 90

# The `## ` headings of the other documents whose sections code cites bare,
# with a leading `N. ` stripped: CLAUDE.md's, the ROADMAP's, the site README's.
# **THE `continue` HERE WAS A SILENCE, AND IT WAS REPLACED ON 2026-09-12.** The
# three paths are written into this function, so a file it cannot open is always
# a defect and never a legitimate case: either one of the three moved, or the
# working tree is not where this suite thinks it is. Swallowing that left the
# heading set short and the `anchors` check then reported as UNKNOWN the sections
# of whichever document had gone missing — a red on innocent files, naming the
# wrong thing, which is worse than the red it replaced. The empty name is what
# `anchors` reports, and it stops the walk instead of shrinking it.
# The sentinel `other_headings` returns in place of a heading set it could not
# read. It is a string nobody's `§` can name, so `anchors` reports it as the
# unknown anchor it is, with the path attached.
constant MISSING_DOCUMENT: str
    "a document this suite could not open: "

function other_headings() -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for path in ["CLAUDE.md", "docs/ROADMAP.md", "site/README.md"]
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            return [MISSING_DOCUMENT + path]

        for line in strings.lines(text.must())
            if strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: "## ")
                out @ out.push(
                    strings.trimmed(spec_text.without_number(strings.without_prefix(text: line, prefix: "## ")))
                )

    return out

function window_start(i: i64) -> i64
    if i < 32
        return 0
    return i - 32

function shortest(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64
    if a < b
        return a
    return b

# Whether `rest` opens with a section number in range or with a section's
# title. `Failure: `T?`` is cited as `Failure`, so a title matches up to its
# colon as well as whole.
function names_a_section(rest: [str]) -> bool
    if rest.len() == 0
        return false

    if spec_text.is_digit(rest[0])
        digits: [str] @ []
        i: i64 @ 0

        while i < rest.len() && spec_text.is_digit(rest[i])
            digits @ digits.push(rest[i])
            i @ i + 1

        # `spec § 4.19` is design.md's numbering written against the wrong
        # document: a dotted number is nobody's section here.
        if i + 1 < rest.len() && rest[i] == "." && spec_text.is_digit(rest[i + 1])
            return false
        n = strings.to_number(digits.join(""))
        return !n.is_err() && n.must() >= 1 && n.must() <= SECTIONS.len()
    text = rest.join("")

    for title in SECTIONS
        if strings.starts_with(text: text, prefix: title) || strings.starts_with(text: text, prefix: before_colon(title))
            return true

    # A title wrapped at the end of a comment line arrives cut, `Strings,` or
    # `Functions and`; a cut of three characters or more that opens a title is
    # taken as that title, because this check reads one line at a time.
    head = strings.trimmed(text)

    if head.len() >= 3
        for title in SECTIONS
            if strings.starts_with(text: title, prefix: head)
                return true

    return false

function before_colon(title: str) -> str
    at = strings.index_of(text: title, needle: ":")

    if at.is_err()
        return title
    return title.slice(from: 0, to: at.must())

# Every quoted name after `marker` on its own line — the compiler's table,
# read from the compiler's own source.
function names_in_table(path: str, marker: str) -> [str]
    text = read_file(path)

    if text.is_err()
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    found: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: marker)

        if at.is_err()
            continue
        quoted = first_quoted(text: row, from: at.must() + marker.len())

        if !quoted.is_err()
            found @ found.push(quoted.must())

    return found

# Every quoted word BEFORE `marker` on its own line — the match arms, where
# one arm may carry several words: `"null" | "nil" | "None" =>`.
# THE MARKER IDENTIFIES THE ARM, AND THE ARROW IS WHERE THE WORD STOPS —
# and those are two different lines when the formatter breaks the arm.
#
# This read `=> ok(ForeignWord(` on ONE line until 2026-08-28. Then
# `WIDTH` went from 88 to 120, `keywords.hero` was swept, and the same
# arm became
#
#     "int" => ok(
#         ForeignWord(message: "...", repair: .swap(word: "i64"))
#     )
#
# so nothing matched and this suite reported **0 refused words out of 23**
# — which is what its own failure message predicted would happen, in
# those words. It went red honestly, so the premise gets a narrower one
# rather than a wider marker: `=> ok(` alone would also match every
# `"record" => ok(.kw_record)` in the same file and report real keywords
# as foreign, which is a FALSE POSITIVE and worse than the red.
#
# What is stable is that the arm's value names `ForeignWord` and the
# arm's pattern sits before the nearest `=>` at or above it. Both shapes
# are read: the arrow on this line, or on the line before.
function words_in_arms(path: str, marker: str) -> [str]
    text = read_file(path)

    if text.is_err()
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    found: [str] @ []
    previous: str @ ""

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: marker)
        source_row: str @ row
        arrow: i64 @ -1

        if !at.is_err()
            arrow @ at.must()
        else

            # The arm was broken across lines: its value names the type
            # here and its pattern is on the line above.
            names_the_type = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: "ForeignWord(")
            arrow_above = strings.index_of(text: previous, needle: "=>")

            if !names_the_type.is_err() && !arrow_above.is_err()
                source_row @ previous
                arrow @ arrow_above.must()
        previous @ row

        if arrow < 0
            continue
        head = source_row.slice(from: 0, to: arrow)
        cursor: i64 @ 0

        while cursor < head.len()
            quoted = first_quoted(text: head, from: cursor)

            if quoted.is_err()
                cursor @ head.len()
            else
                found @ found.push(quoted.must())

                # Past the closing quote: two characters for the quotes
                # themselves plus what they held.
                cursor @ cursor + advance_past(text: head, from: cursor)

    return found

# The text of the first `"…"` at or after `from`.
function first_quoted(text: str, from: i64) -> str?
    opens: i64 @ from

    while opens < text.len() && text[opens] != 34
        opens @ opens + 1

    if opens >= text.len()
        return fail(code: "unquoted", msg: "no quoted text here")
    closes: i64 @ opens + 1

    while closes < text.len() && text[closes] != 34
        closes @ closes + 1

    if closes >= text.len()
        return fail(code: "unquoted", msg: "the quote is not closed")
    return ok(text.slice(from: opens + 1, to: closes))

# How far past `from` the first quoted run ends — the cursor step that keeps
# `words_in_arms` moving forward.
function advance_past(text: str, from: i64) -> i64
    i: i64 @ from
    seen: i64 @ 0

    while i < text.len() && seen < 2
        if text[i] == 34
            seen @ seen + 1
        i @ i + 1

    return i - from

# The record is cross-checked against what the tool prints (panel 123 R5).
#
# **Whether it is under the ceiling is the headroom question in `budget` above,
# and it is not asked twice here** — one rule in one place. What this asks is
# the pair the old instrument could not: does the tool print the same record
# this file holds, model id and date included, and does it still call THAT the
# binding number rather than the vendored maximum.
function recorded(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str)
    said = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "measure", SPEC])

    if said.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/real", detail: "  `measure " + SPEC + "`: " + shell.refusal(said))
        return
    row = REAL_TOKENS.to_str() + "   " + REAL_MODEL + ", " + REAL_TAKEN

    if !strings.contains(text: said.must().out, needle: row)
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/real",
            detail: "  `measure " + SPEC + "` does not print \"" + row + "\".\n  The record is in two places, this file and `selfhost/cli/measure.hero`, for\n  the reason CEILING is: one printed and one checked. Whichever moved, the\n  other moves with it (defect 017's class), and the model id and the date\n  travel WITH the number because a count without them is not a measurement."
        )
        return

    if !strings.contains(text: said.must().out, needle: "the binding number")
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/real",
            detail: "  `measure " + SPEC + "` prints no row calling the reader's count binding.\n  Until 2026-09-09 it called the VENDORED maximum that, and the vendored\n  maximum is OpenAI's tokeniser: the label is what a reader trusted, and it\n  cost 998 tokens (docs/measurements/023)."
        )
        return

    # **The contract's record, held to the same standard since 2026-09-12.** It
    # was not held to any: the contract had no real count at all, and `measure`
    # printed no `real` row for it, which is how it sat 1806 tokens over a
    # ceiling while reporting nine to spare (panel 134).
    told = shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "measure", CONTRACT])

    if told.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/real", detail: "  `measure " + CONTRACT + "`: " + shell.refusal(told))
        return
    contract_row = CONTRACT_REAL_TOKENS.to_str() + "   " + REAL_MODEL + ", " + CONTRACT_REAL_TAKEN

    if !strings.contains(text: told.must().out, needle: contract_row)
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/real",
            detail: "  `measure " + CONTRACT + "` does not print \"" + contract_row + "\".\n  The contract's record lives in this file and in\n  `selfhost/measure/pinned.hero`, the way the spec's does, and whichever moved\n  the other moves with it. Take a new one with\n  `heroes measure " + CONTRACT + " --refresh` and paste what it prints."
        )
        return
    report.keep_pass(@r)

## The fourth copy

# The editor's grammar against the compiler's own tables: the escapes it paints
# as legal, and the words it paints as keywords.
#
# Both sides are READ rather than pinned, so this holds two files together
# instead of adding a third place for the truth to live — which is the rule the
# top of this file opens with.
function colouring(@r: report.Report)
    text = read_file(GRAMMAR)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/colouring", detail: "  cannot read " + GRAMMAR)
        return
    want = compiler_escapes(ESCAPE_SOURCE)

    if !floors.sound(@r, label: "spec/colouring", found: want.len(), floor: LEAST_ESCAPES, what: "escapes read out of " + ESCAPE_SOURCE)
        return
    escapes_agree(
        @r,
        want: for_context(want: want, delimiter: "\""),
        got: class_members(text: text.must(), marker: "constant.character.escape.hero"),
        where: "a string"
    )
    escapes_agree(
        @r,
        want: for_context(want: want, delimiter: "'"),
        got: class_members(text: text.must(), marker: "constant.character.hero"),
        where: "a character literal"
    )
    reserved = compiler_keywords(KEYWORD_SOURCE)

    if !floors.sound(@r, label: "spec/colouring", found: reserved.len(), floor: LEAST_KEYWORDS, what: "keyword arms read out of " + KEYWORD_SOURCE)
        return
    painted = alternatives(text: text.must(), marker: "keyword.control.hero")
    absent = absent_from(want: reserved, got: painted)
    extra = absent_from(want: painted, got: reserved)

    if absent.len() == 0 && extra.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "spec/colouring",
        detail: "  the editor's grammar and " + KEYWORD_SOURCE + " disagree about what a keyword is.\n  the compiler reserves and the grammar leaves plain: " + absent.join(" ") + "\n  the grammar paints and the compiler does not reserve: " + extra.join(" ") + "\n  The first kind leaves a real keyword uncoloured. The SECOND is the one that\n  hurts: a word the grammar paints as a keyword and the lexer does not reserve\n  is a legal identifier shown to the author as syntax, so a correct program\n  reads as one that cannot be."
    )

# The braces of an `f` literal, the editor's grammar against the compiler's
# lexer (panel 184 R1, defect 173, 2026-10-02): the pairs the grammar paints
# as escapes are the pairs the lexer reads as one brace each, `{{` and `}}`
# both, and the brace it paints alone as an error is the one the lexer
# refuses, `lone_brace`. The lexer is asked by lexing each brace inside an
# `f` literal, the grammar by its own rules, so neither side is pinned here.
# The grammar had no rule for the form until that day: one painting a pair the
# lexer refuses shows a mistake as legal, and one painting a brace the lexer
# reads shows a correct program as an error, this check's first case again.
function braces(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str)
    text = read_file(GRAMMAR)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/colouring", detail: "  cannot read " + GRAMMAR)
        return
    pairs = regex_alternatives(text: text.must(), marker: "constant.character.escape.brace.hero")
    lone = regex_alternatives(text: text.must(), marker: "invalid.illegal.lone-brace.hero")
    wrong: [str] @ []

    for brace in ["{", "}"]
        doubled = brace + brace
        said = lexed_in_piece(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, brace: doubled)

        if said.is_err() || said.must().code != 0
            wrong @ wrong.push("the lexer does not read `" + doubled + "` as one brace inside an `f` literal")

        if absent_from(want: [doubled], got: pairs).len() > 0
            wrong @ wrong.push("the grammar does not paint `" + doubled + "` as one brace")

    for pair in pairs
        said = lexed_in_piece(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, brace: pair)

        if said.is_err() || said.must().code != 0
            wrong @ wrong.push("the grammar paints `" + pair + "` as an escape, and the lexer refuses it")

    if lone != ["}"]
        wrong @ wrong.push(
            "the grammar's lone brace is `" + lone.join("|") + "`, where `}` is the one the lexer refuses"
        )
    refused = lexed_in_piece(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, brace: "}")

    if refused.is_err() || refused.must().code != 1 || !strings.contains(text: refused.must().err, needle: "[lone_brace]")
        wrong @ wrong.push("the lexer does not refuse a `}` alone inside an `f` literal as `lone_brace`")

    if wrong.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/colouring", detail: "  " + wrong.join("\n  "))

# What the lexer says of `brace` written inside an `f` literal, between two
# letters, alone in a program of its own under `scratch`.
function lexed_in_piece(scratch: str, compiler: str, brace: str) -> shell.Run?
    path = scratch + "/spec-braces.hero"
    write_file(path: path, text: "function main()\n    print(f\"a" + brace + "b\")\n")?
    return shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "lex", path])

## The escapes the spec names, run through the grammar

# Every escape § 2 names, and the other spellings of its escape by code, asked
# of the lexer in each of the three literals an escape stands in and of the
# grammar's rules for that literal, RUN (defect 377): the grammar paints one
# escape exactly where the lexer reads one.
#
# `colouring` above compares the members of the first `[…]` of a rule's
# `match` with `escape_text`'s arms, which sees a class of letters and nothing
# else. Panel 192's R5 put `\u{1b}` in the language and its rule beside the
# first in two of the grammar's literals, and no check read either: the lane
# that landed it tested the pattern with Python's `re` alone (its report,
# 2026-10-06). A pattern is a program, so this runs it (`textmate.hero`), and
# what it is held to is read rather than pinned: the escapes are the spec's
# own, every code span of § 2 that opens with a backslash, and the verdict on
# each is the compiler's lexer's, by lexing a program that holds it.
#
# The other spellings are made from the spec's own `\u{…}`, and the lexer must
# refuse each, the spec giving that escape one spelling: its digits in
# capitals, a leading zero, no digits, no closing brace, no braces, seven
# digits. Each is a way the pattern can break: a class admitting capitals, a
# first digit admitting 0, a count with no ceiling, a brace made optional.
#
# **The lexer gives three verdicts, and the grammar is held to two of them**
# (2026-10-07). It reads an escape (exit 0); it refuses a code spelled the
# one way that names a character a string writes otherwise (`\u{41}`, which
# a string writes `A`), `escape_not_needed` and nothing else, which the grammar
# paints as the escape it is, which characters a string must write by code
# being the compiler's to say (the rule's own comment, panel 192's R5); or it
# refuses what is no escape at all, which the grammar must not paint. The
# grammar painted `\u{d800}`, a surrogate, and `\u{110000}`, past Unicode, as
# escapes until that day, the lexer refusing both `unknown_escape`, and no
# probe asked: `CODE_EDGES` below are the edges of what a code may name.
constant LEAST_SPEC_ESCAPES: i64
    7

# The edges of a code that names a character, a Unicode scalar value: the
# last code before the surrogates, the first and the last surrogate, the first
# code after them, the first of five digits, the last scalar value and the
# first past it. Facts of Unicode and not of the spec, so they are written
# here; what the lexer says of each is asked, as of every other probe.
constant CODE_EDGES: [str]
    [
        "\\u{d7ff}"
        "\\u{d800}"
        "\\u{dfff}"
        "\\u{e000}"
        "\\u{10000}"
        "\\u{10ffff}"
        "\\u{110000}"
    ]

# The literals an escape stands in, by the name of the grammar's repository
# entry that paints its text: a string, an `f` literal's text, a character
# literal.
constant LITERALS: [str]
    [
        "string"
        "interpolated"
        "char"
    ]

# What the lexer said of one escape in one literal: `read` where it reads it,
# `named` where it refuses an escape by code only as a character a string
# writes otherwise.
record Lexed
    escape: str
    literal: str
    read: bool
    named: bool

function escapes_painted(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str, spec: str)
    grammar = read_file(GRAMMAR)

    if grammar.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: "spec/colouring", detail: "  cannot read " + GRAMMAR)
        return
    spelled = spec_escapes(spec)

    if !floors.sound(@r, label: "spec/colouring", found: spelled.len(), floor: LEAST_SPEC_ESCAPES, what: "escapes named by the code spans of " + SPEC + "'s § 2")
        return
    said: [Lexed] @ []
    probes: [str] @ spelled
    others = other_spellings(spelled)

    for other in others
        probes @ probes.push(other)

    for edge in CODE_EDGES
        probes @ probes.push(edge)

    for escape in probes
        for literal in LITERALS
            ran = lexed_escape(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, literal: literal, escape: escape)

            if ran.is_err() || ran.must().code != 0 && ran.must().code != 1
                report.keep_failure(
                    @r,
                    label: "spec/colouring",
                    detail: "  the lexer gave no verdict on `" + escape + "` in " + literal_said(literal) + ": " + shell.ending(ran)
                )
                return
            said @ said.push(
                Lexed(escape: escape, literal: literal, read: ran.must().code == 0, named: ran.must().code == 1 && strings.starts_with(text: escape, prefix: "\\u{") && only_named(ran.must().err))
            )

    wrong = disagreements(grammar: grammar.must(), spelled: spelled, others: others, said: said)

    if wrong.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "spec/colouring",
            detail: "  the grammar's rules for a literal cannot be run: " + why_unrun(wrong) + "\n  Teach `tests/harness/textmate.hero` the construct, or write the rule without it: a rule the check cannot run is a rule nothing judges."
        )
        return

    if wrong.must().len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "spec/colouring",
        detail: "  " + wrong.must().join("\n  ") + "\n  " + GRAMMAR + "'s rules for each literal were run as an editor runs them,\n  first match wins (`tests/harness/textmate.hero`). An escape the lexer reads\n  and the grammar does not paint falls to the `invalid.illegal` rule, a correct\n  program shown as an error; one the lexer refuses and the grammar paints is a\n  mistake shown as legal."
    )

function why_unrun(got: [str]?) -> str
    return match got
        .ok _  => ""
        .err e => e.msg

# Whether every diagnostic the lexer wrote is `escape_not_needed`, and one
# was. Of an escape by code it says a code spelled the one way, naming a
# character a string writes otherwise; of a letter escape it says something
# else, `\"` in a character literal, an escape that literal does not have,
# so the caller asks it of an escape by code alone.
function only_named(err: str) -> bool
    pieces = strings.split_text(text: err, separator: "error[")

    if pieces.len() < 2
        return false

    for piece in pieces.slice(from: 1, to: pieces.len())
        if !strings.starts_with(text: piece, prefix: "escape_not_needed]")
            return false

    return true

# Where the grammar and the lexer part, one sentence each; an error where a
# rule cannot be run. `spelled` are the escapes the spec names, `others` the
# other spellings of its escape by code, `said` the lexer's verdict on each
# probe in each literal.
function disagreements(grammar: str, spelled: [str], others: [str], said: [Lexed]) -> [str]?
    wrong: [str] @ []

    for literal in LITERALS
        rules = textmate.rules_of(grammar: grammar, name: literal)?

        for one in said
            if one.literal != literal
                continue
            subject: str @ one.escape + "z\")"
            whole: i64 @ one.escape.chars().len()

            if literal == "char"
                subject @ "'" + one.escape + "')"
                whole @ whole + 2
            got = textmate.painted(rules: rules, text: subject)?
            as_escape = strings.starts_with(text: got.scope, prefix: "constant.character") && got.reach == whole

            if one.read && !as_escape
                wrong @ wrong.push(
                    "`" + one.escape + "` in " + literal_said(literal) + ": the lexer reads it, and the grammar paints " + painted_said(got)
                )
            else if one.named && !as_escape
                wrong @ wrong.push(
                    "`" + one.escape + "` in " + literal_said(literal) + ": a code the lexer refuses only as a character a string writes otherwise, `escape_not_needed`, and the grammar paints " + painted_said(got)
                )
            else if !one.read && !one.named && as_escape
                wrong @ wrong.push(
                    "`" + one.escape + "` in " + literal_said(literal) + ": the lexer refuses it as no escape, and the grammar paints it `" + got.scope + "`, an escape"
                )

    for escape in spelled
        read_somewhere: bool @ false

        for one in said
            if one.escape == escape && one.read
                read_somewhere @ true

        if !read_somewhere
            wrong @ wrong.push("the spec names `" + escape + "`, and the lexer reads it in no literal")

    for one in said
        if one.read && absent_from(want: [one.escape], got: others).len() == 0
            wrong @ wrong.push(
                "`" + one.escape + "` is read in " + literal_said(one.literal) + ", a second spelling of an escape the spec gives one"
            )

    return ok(wrong)

function painted_said(got: textmate.Painted) -> str
    if got.scope.len() == 0
        return "no rule over it"
    return "`" + got.scope + "` over " + got.reach.to_str() + " characters"

function literal_said(literal: str) -> str
    if literal == "interpolated"
        return "an `f` literal's text"

    if literal == "char"
        return "a character literal"
    return "a string"

# Every code span of § 2 that opens with a backslash, each once, in the order
# met: the escapes the spec names, read where it names them.
function spec_escapes(spec: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    at = spec_text.section_named(text: spec, name: "Literals")

    if at.is_err()
        return out
    rows = strings.lines(spec.slice(from: at.must(), to: spec.len()))
    kept_rows: [str] @ []
    in_fence: bool @ false

    for row in rows.slice(from: 1, to: rows.len())
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "## ")
            break

        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "```")
            in_fence @ !in_fence
            continue

        if !in_fence
            kept_rows @ kept_rows.push(row)

    pieces = strings.split_text(text: kept_rows.join("\n"), separator: "`")
    i: i64 @ 1

    while i < pieces.len()
        span = pieces[i]

        if strings.starts_with(text: span, prefix: "\\") && span.len() >= 2 && absent_from(want: [span], got: out).len() == 1
            out @ out.push(span)
        i @ i + 2

    return out

# The other spellings of each `\u{…}` in `spelled`, made from its digits: in
# capitals, after a zero, none, without the closing brace, without either
# brace, and run to seven. None of them is one of `spelled`.
function other_spellings(spelled: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for escape in spelled
        if !strings.starts_with(text: escape, prefix: "\\u{") || !strings.ends_with(text: escape, suffix: "}")
            continue
        digits = escape.slice(from: 3, to: escape.len() - 1)
        made: [str] @ [
            "\\u{" + capitals(digits) + "}"
            "\\u{0" + digits + "}"
            "\\u{}"
            "\\u{" + digits
            "\\u" + digits
        ]

        if digits.len() < 7
            made @ made.push("\\u{" + digits + "0".repeat((7 - digits.len()).to_u64().must()) + "}")

        for other in made
            if absent_from(want: [other], got: spelled).len() == 1 && absent_from(want: [other], got: out).len() == 1
                out @ out.push(other)

    return out

function capitals(text: str) -> str
    out: str @ ""

    for c in text.chars()
        at = strings.index_of(text: "abcdef", needle: c)

        if at.is_err()
            out @ out + c
        else
            out @ out + "ABCDEF".slice(from: at.must(), to: at.must() + 1)

    return out

# What the lexer says of `escape` inside one literal, alone in a program of its
# own under `scratch`: between two letters in a string or an `f` literal, `z`
# after it so no digit of a code can run on, alone between the quotes of a
# character literal.
function lexed_escape(scratch: str, compiler: str, literal: str, escape: str) -> shell.Run?
    path = scratch + "/spec-escape.hero"
    write_file(path: path, text: escape_program(literal: literal, escape: escape))?
    return shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: compiler, words: [compiler, "lex", path])

function escape_program(literal: str, escape: str) -> str
    if literal == "char"
        return "function main()\n    print('" + escape + "')\n"
    opener: str @ "\""

    if literal == "interpolated"
        opener @ "f\""
    return "function main()\n    print(" + opener + "a" + escape + "z\")\n"

# The alternatives of a rule's `"match"` whose whole pattern is one or more
# literal texts joined by `|`, each with its regular expression's backslashes
# taken off: `\\{\\{|\\}\\}` in the JSON reads as `{{` and `}}`.
function regex_alternatives(text: str, marker: str) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []
    source = json_unescape(match_value(text: text, marker: marker))
    opens = strings.index_of(text: source, needle: "\"")

    if opens.is_err()
        return found
    rest = source.slice(from: opens.must() + 1, to: source.len())
    closes = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "\"")

    if closes.is_err()
        return found

    for one in strings.split_text(text: rest.slice(from: 0, to: closes.must()), separator: "|")
        cs = one.chars()
        plain: str @ ""

        for c in cs
            if c != "\\"
                plain @ plain + c

        if plain.len() > 0
            found @ found.push(plain)

    return found

# One comparison, in one direction, said twice.
function escapes_agree(@r: report.Report, want: [str], got: [str], where: str)
    absent = absent_from(want: want, got: got)
    extra = absent_from(want: got, got: want)

    if absent.len() == 0 && extra.len() == 0
        report.keep_pass(@r)
        return
    report.keep_failure(
        @r,
        label: "spec/colouring",
        detail: "  in " + where + " " + GRAMMAR + " admits the escapes `" + got.join("` `") + "` and " + ESCAPE_SOURCE + " accepts `" + want.join("` `") + "`.\n  missing from the grammar: " + absent.join(" ") + "\n  admitted by the grammar and not by the compiler: " + extra.join(" ") + "\n  An escape the compiler accepts and the grammar omits does not go UNCOLOURED:\n  it falls through to the `invalid.illegal` rule below it, so a correct program\n  is painted as an error in the editor. That is what `\\r` did from the day the\n  grammar was written until 2026-09-13, and `print(\"a\\rb\")` runs at exit 0."
    )

# What is in `want` and not in `got`.
function absent_from(want: [str], got: [str]) -> [str]
    here: {str: bool} @ {}

    for one in got
        here[one] @ true

    out: [str] @ []

    for one in want
        if here[one].is_err()
            out @ out.push(one)

    return out

# EVERY escape the compiler accepts, read out of `escape_text`'s own arms — the
# rows read `if after_backslash == "n"` and `if after_backslash == "\\"`.
#
# **All six, not the three letters.** This took only lowercase letters until
# 2026-09-14, on the argument that the backslash and the two quotes are
# structural and spelled differently in a regular expression than in Heroes
# source. Panel 146's review refuted the argument by running it: dropping `\\`
# and the quote from BOTH grammar classes left this check at **20 passed, 0
# failed** while `"a\\b"`, `"a\"b"` and `'\''` were all painted
# `invalid.illegal` — and **swapping the quotes between the two contexts** did
# the same, which is panel 008's whole split undetected. Half of § 2's six
# escapes were outside the instrument.
#
# The notations do differ, and that is handled by comparing SETS of the one
# character after the backslash rather than the spelling around it: the Heroes
# source writes `"\\\\"` for a backslash and the JSON writes `\\\\` inside a
# class, and both denote the single character this reads.
function compiler_escapes(path: str) -> [str]
    text = read_file(path)

    if text.is_err()
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    marker = "if after_backslash == \""
    found: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: marker)

        if at.is_err()
            continue
        rest = row.slice(from: at.must() + marker.len(), to: row.len())

        if rest.len() == 0
            continue
        cs = rest.chars()
        one = cs[0]

        # TWO characters, not one, and that is the whole of this arm: in the
        # Heroes source a backslash is written `\\` and a quote `\"`, so both
        # arms OPEN with a backslash and reading one character reports the
        # quote arm as a backslash. Measured the hour this was widened.
        if one == "\\"
            if cs.len() < 2
                continue

            if cs[1] == "\\"
                found @ found.push("\\")
            else if cs[1] == "\""
                found @ found.push("\"")
            continue

        if strings.contains(text: LOWER, needle: one) || one == "'"
            found @ found.push(one)

    return found

# One JSON string's escapes undone, so what is read afterwards is the regular
# expression the editor compiles rather than the bytes the file stores.
#
# `\\` becomes one backslash and `\"` becomes one quote, which is the whole of
# what a TextMate `match` uses. Without this pass the class for a string reads
# as five backslashes and a quote, and any comparison against the compiler's
# own table would be comparing two notations rather than two claims.
function json_unescape(text: str) -> str
    cs = text.chars()
    out: str @ ""
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < cs.len()
        if cs[i] == "\\" && i + 1 < cs.len()
            out @ out + cs[i + 1]
            i @ i + 2
            continue
        out @ out + cs[i]
        i @ i + 1

    return out

# The escapes legal in ONE context, from the set the compiler accepts.
#
# `escape_text` gates the two quotes on the context — `"` only in a string, `'`
# only in a character literal — so the other one is dropped here. Without this
# the check could not see the quotes SWAPPED between the two classes, which the
# review demonstrated passing at 20/0.
function for_context(want: [str], delimiter: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    for one in want
        if one == "\"" || one == "'"
            if one == delimiter
                out @ out.push(one)
            continue
        out @ out.push(one)

    return out

# Every word the lexer's own table calls a keyword: `"constant" => ok(.kw_constant)`.
function compiler_keywords(path: str) -> [str]
    text = read_file(path)

    if text.is_err()
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    found: [str] @ []

    for row in strings.lines(text.must())
        at = strings.index_of(text: row, needle: "=> ok(.kw_")

        if at.is_err()
            continue
        quoted = first_quoted(text: row.slice(from: 0, to: at.must()), from: 0)

        if !quoted.is_err()
            found @ found.push(quoted.must())

    return found

# The `"match"` VALUE of the rule that `marker` names, and nothing else.
#
# **This is the repair panel 146's review paid for, and it is worth its own
# paragraph.** Until 2026-09-14 the two readers below took the line PHYSICALLY
# ABOVE the scope name, on the true observation that the string rule writes its
# `name` on the next line and the character rule on the same one. That bound the
# instrument to the file's hand-formatting rather than to its JSON, and the
# review ran all three consequences:
#
#   - merging the string rule's `"match"` and `"name"` onto one line — the style
#     the character rule three lines below ALREADY uses, and a change `json.load`
#     confirms is semantics-preserving — made the reader take the rule's
#     `"comment"`, whose prose quotes the historical class, and report a correct
#     grammar RED with a message quoting a sentence;
#   - expanding the character rule the other way made the reader take the
#     `"name"` line, which holds no bracket, and report every escape missing;
#   - and with the comment prose refreshed to name today's class and the real
#     regex broken back, the suite read **20 passed, 0 failed** while `"a\rb"`
#     scoped through that same file came out `invalid.illegal.unknown-escape`.
#     That is the defect this check exists to catch, back, with the instrument
#     green — a check written against silent rot, rotting silently.
#
# Keying on `"match":` cannot read a comment, because a comment's key is
# `"comment":`, and it is layout-free: the value may sit on the marker's own
# line or on any line above it, stopping at the rule's own opening brace so a
# rule with no `match` cannot borrow the previous rule's.
function match_value(text: str, marker: str) -> str
    rows = strings.lines(text)
    at: i64 @ -1
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < rows.len()
        if !strings.index_of(text: rows[i], needle: marker).is_err()
            at @ i
            i @ rows.len()
        else
            i @ i + 1

    if at < 0
        return ""
    key = "\"match\":"
    back: i64 @ at

    while back >= 0
        found = strings.index_of(text: rows[back], needle: key)

        if !found.is_err()
            return rows[back].slice(from: found.must() + key.len(), to: rows[back].len())

        # A rule's own opening brace ends the search. Without it a rule that has
        # `begin`/`end` and no `match` would read the rule above it.
        if back < at && strings.contains(text: rows[back], needle: "{")
            return ""
        back @ back - 1

    return ""

# The members of the first `[…]` in that rule's `"match"`, as single characters.
#
# A doubled backslash in the JSON is one backslash in the regular expression, so
# `\\\\` reads as one member and not two.
function class_members(text: str, marker: str) -> [str]
    found: [str] @ []
    source = json_unescape(match_value(text: text, marker: marker))
    opens = strings.index_of(text: source, needle: "[")

    if opens.is_err()
        return found
    rest = source.slice(from: opens.must() + 1, to: source.len())
    closes = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: "]")

    if closes.is_err()
        return found
    cs = rest.slice(from: 0, to: closes.must()).chars()
    i: i64 @ 0

    while i < cs.len()
        one = cs[i]

        if one == "\\"
            if i + 1 < cs.len() && cs[i + 1] == "\\"
                found @ found.push("\\")
                i @ i + 2
                continue
            i @ i + 1
            continue

        if strings.contains(text: LOWER, needle: one) || one == "\"" || one == "'"
            found @ found.push(one)
        i @ i + 1

    return found

# The alternatives inside the first `(…)` of that rule's `"match"`, which for a
# TextMate grammar is `\b(constant|function|…)\b`.
function alternatives(text: str, marker: str) -> [str]
    none: [str] = []
    source = json_unescape(match_value(text: text, marker: marker))
    opens = strings.index_of(text: source, needle: "(")

    if opens.is_err()
        return none
    rest = source.slice(from: opens.must() + 1, to: source.len())
    closes = strings.index_of(text: rest, needle: ")")

    if closes.is_err()
        return none
    return strings.split_text(text: rest.slice(from: 0, to: closes.must()), separator: "|")

test "the grammar's rules are read out of their match, in either layout"
    # The two layouts that already coexist in `heroes.tmLanguage.json`: the
    # string rule writes its `name` on the NEXT line, the character rule on the
    # SAME one. Both must read alike, because each is the other's counter-example
    # and a reformat between them is semantics-preserving.
    # THE REAL FILE, because writing its class as a Heroes literal would need
    # every backslash doubled twice over and the test would then be about the
    # transcription rather than about the reader.
    real = read_file(GRAMMAR)
    assert !real.is_err()
    assert class_members(text: real.must(), marker: "constant.character.escape.hero") == ["n", "t", "r", "\\", "\""]
    assert class_members(text: real.must(), marker: "constant.character.hero") == ["n", "t", "r", "\\", "'"]
    assert absent_from(want: ["constant", "function", "record", "variant", "nullptr"], got: alternatives(text: real.must(), marker: "keyword.control.hero")).len() == 0

    # THE REGRESSION PANEL 146 PAID FOR, on a class simple enough to read at a
    # glance. Three layouts of one rule: `match` above `name`, the two merged
    # onto one line, and a comment above both whose prose quotes a DIFFERENT
    # class. All three must give the same answer, and none may give the
    # comment's.
    split = "        {\n          \"comment\": \"this prose quotes the old class [xy] and must never be read\",\n          \"match\": \"[ntr]\",\n          \"name\": \"constant.character.escape.hero\"\n        }\n"
    merged = "        {\n          \"comment\": \"this prose quotes the old class [xy] and must never be read\",\n          \"match\": \"[ntr]\", \"name\": \"constant.character.escape.hero\"\n        }\n"
    bare = "        { \"match\": \"[ntr]\", \"name\": \"constant.character.escape.hero\" },\n"
    assert class_members(text: split, marker: "constant.character.escape.hero") == ["n", "t", "r"]
    assert class_members(text: merged, marker: "constant.character.escape.hero") == ["n", "t", "r"]
    assert class_members(text: bare, marker: "constant.character.escape.hero") == ["n", "t", "r"]

    # A rule that is not there reads as nothing rather than as agreement, which
    # is what the floor above it exists to catch.
    assert class_members(text: split, marker: "no.such.scope").len() == 0
    assert alternatives(text: split, marker: "no.such.scope").len() == 0

    # And a scope whose rule carries no `match` reads as nothing rather than
    # borrowing the rule above it: the search stops at the opening brace.
    beginning = "        { \"match\": \"[ntr]\", \"name\": \"other.scope\" },\n        {\n          \"begin\": \"q\",\n          \"name\": \"string.quoted.double.hero\"\n        }\n"
    assert class_members(text: beginning, marker: "string.quoted.double.hero").len() == 0

test "a rule's literal alternatives are read with their backslashes off, and a rule not there reads as none"
    rows = "        { \"match\": \"\\\\{\\\\{|\\\\}\\\\}\", \"name\": \"constant.character.escape.brace.hero\" },\n        { \"match\": \"\\\\}\", \"name\": \"invalid.illegal.lone-brace.hero\" },\n"
    assert regex_alternatives(text: rows, marker: "constant.character.escape.brace.hero") == ["{{", "}}"]
    assert regex_alternatives(text: rows, marker: "invalid.illegal.lone-brace.hero") == ["}"]
    assert regex_alternatives(text: rows, marker: "no.such.scope").len() == 0

test "the escapes § 2 names are read from its code spans, and the other spellings of the one by code are made from it"
    text = "## 1. Files\n`\\q` is no escape of § 2\n## 2. Literals\n- Two escapes: `\\n` and `\\u{7f}`, and `x` is not one; `\\n` again.\n\n```\nprint(\"`\\\\t`\")\n```\n## 3. Types\n`\\t` is not in § 2\n"
    assert spec_escapes(text) == ["\\n", "\\u{7f}"]
    assert other_spellings(["\\n", "\\u{7f}"]) == ["\\u{7F}", "\\u{07f}", "\\u{}", "\\u{7f", "\\u7f", "\\u{7f00000}"]

    # Digits with no letter have no spelling in capitals: that would be the
    # escape itself, which is not another spelling.
    assert other_spellings(["\\u{85}"]) == ["\\u{085}", "\\u{}", "\\u{85", "\\u85", "\\u{8500000}"]
    assert escape_program(literal: "interpolated", escape: "\\n") == "function main()\n    print(f\"a\\nz\")\n"
    assert escape_program(literal: "char", escape: "\\'") == "function main()\n    print('\\'')\n"
    real = read_file(SPEC)
    assert !real.is_err()
    assert spec_escapes(real.must()).len() >= LEAST_SPEC_ESCAPES

test "the grammar paints an escape exactly where the lexer reads one, and each way to break the pattern is red"
    # THE REAL FILE against what § 2 says the lexer reads, so the comparison is
    # tested on the rules an editor runs; the suite asks the lexer itself.
    real = read_file(GRAMMAR)
    assert !real.is_err()
    spelled = ["\\n", "\\t", "\\r", "\\\\", "\\\"", "\\'", "\\u{1b}"]
    others = other_spellings(spelled)
    said = said_by_section_two(spelled)
    assert disagreements(grammar: real.must(), spelled: spelled, others: others, said: said).must().len() == 0

    # Each way the escape by code's pattern can break, and the sentence that
    # names it: a class admitting capitals, a first digit admitting 0, a count
    # with no ceiling, a surrogate admitted, a code past Unicode admitted, a
    # rule that matches nothing, and one that paints fewer codes than name a
    # character.
    capitals_wrong = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]{0,2}",
        now: "[1-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]{0,2}",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: capitals_wrong, needle: "`\\u{1B}` in a string: the lexer refuses it as no escape")
    assert strings.contains(
        text: capitals_wrong,
        needle: "`\\u{1B}` in an `f` literal's text: the lexer refuses it as no escape"
    )
    zero_wrong = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]{0,2}",
        now: "[0-9a-f][0-9a-f]{0,2}",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: zero_wrong, needle: "`\\u{01b}` in a string: the lexer refuses it as no escape")
    ceiling_wrong = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]{4}",
        now: "[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]*",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(
        text: ceiling_wrong,
        needle: "`\\u{1b00000}` in a string: the lexer refuses it as no escape"
    )
    surrogate_wrong = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "[1-9a-ce-f][0-9a-f]{3}",
        now: "[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]{3}",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: surrogate_wrong, needle: "`\\u{d800}` in a string: the lexer refuses it as no escape")
    assert strings.contains(
        text: surrogate_wrong,
        needle: "`\\u{dfff}` in an `f` literal's text: the lexer refuses it as no escape"
    )
    past_wrong = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "10[0-9a-f]{4}",
        now: "[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]{5}",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: past_wrong, needle: "`\\u{110000}` in a string: the lexer refuses it as no escape")
    never = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "|10[0-9a-f]{4})",
        now: "|10[0-9a-f]{4})x",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(
        text: never,
        needle: "`\\u{1b}` in a string: the lexer reads it, and the grammar paints `invalid.illegal.unknown-escape.hero` over 2 characters"
    )
    fewer = said_of_broken(grammar: real.must(), was: "|[1-9a-f][0-9a-f]{4}|", now: "|", others: others, said: said)
    assert strings.contains(
        text: fewer,
        needle: "`\\u{10000}` in a string: a code the lexer refuses only as a character a string writes otherwise"
    )

    # The `f` literal's own rules, which no check read until this one: its
    # escapes painted as text is red, as the string's would be.
    piece = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "constant.character.escape.interpolated.hero",
        now: "string.quoted.double.interpolated.hero",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: piece, needle: "`\\n` in an `f` literal's text: the lexer reads it")

    # The other direction: a character literal the lexer refuses painted as one.
    leak = said_of_broken(
        grammar: real.must(),
        was: "invalid.illegal.char-literal.hero",
        now: "constant.character.hero",
        others: others,
        said: said
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: leak, needle: "`\\u{1b}` in a character literal: the lexer refuses it as no escape")

    # And a rule the reader cannot run is an error, never a pass.
    lazy = strings.split_text(text: real.must(), separator: "'[^']*'").join("'[^']*?'")
    assert disagreements(grammar: lazy, spelled: spelled, others: others, said: said).is_err()

    # The lexer's side is checked too: a spelled escape no literal reads, and a
    # second spelling one does, are each a sentence.
    nowhere: [Lexed] @ []

    for one in said
        if one.escape == "\\t"
            nowhere @ nowhere.push(Lexed(escape: one.escape, literal: one.literal, read: false, named: false))
        else if one.escape == "\\u{1B}"
            nowhere @ nowhere.push(Lexed(escape: one.escape, literal: one.literal, read: true, named: false))
        else
            nowhere @ nowhere.push(one)

    unread = disagreements(grammar: real.must(), spelled: spelled, others: others, said: nowhere).must().join("\n")
    assert strings.contains(text: unread, needle: "the spec names `\\t`, and the lexer reads it in no literal")
    assert strings.contains(
        text: unread,
        needle: "`\\u{1B}` is read in a string, a second spelling of an escape the spec gives one"
    )

# What § 2 says the lexer reads, as verdicts: the four letter escapes in every
# literal, `\"` and the escape by code in the two string literals, `\'` in a
# character literal alone, the other spellings of the escape by code nowhere,
# and of the edges of a code, those naming a character refused in the two
# string literals only as a character a string writes otherwise, and a
# surrogate and a code past Unicode as no escape.
function said_by_section_two(spelled: [str]) -> [Lexed]
    out: [Lexed] @ []
    probes: [str] @ spelled

    for other in other_spellings(spelled)
        probes @ probes.push(other)

    for edge in CODE_EDGES
        probes @ probes.push(edge)

    for escape in probes
        for literal in LITERALS
            read: bool @ false
            by_code: bool @ false

            if escape == "\\n" || escape == "\\t" || escape == "\\r" || escape == "\\\\"
                read @ true
            else if escape == "\\\"" || escape == "\\u{1b}"
                read @ literal != "char"
            else if escape == "\\'"
                read @ literal == "char"
            else if escape == "\\u{d7ff}" || escape == "\\u{e000}" || escape == "\\u{10000}" || escape == "\\u{10ffff}"
                by_code @ literal != "char"
            out @ out.push(Lexed(escape: escape, literal: literal, read: read, named: by_code))

    return out

# The sentences `disagreements` says of `grammar` with `was` written `now`,
# joined; `was` must be there, or the case would test the grammar unbroken.
function said_of_broken(grammar: str, was: str, now: str, others: [str], said: [Lexed]) -> str
    assert strings.contains(text: grammar, needle: was)
    spelled = ["\\n", "\\t", "\\r", "\\\\", "\\\"", "\\'", "\\u{1b}"]
    broke = strings.split_text(text: grammar, separator: was).join(now)
    return disagreements(grammar: broke, spelled: spelled, others: others, said: said).must().join("\n")

test "the two sides of the colouring check are read out of the compiler's own source"
    # Not pinned here: read from the files, so this test dies with a rename
    # rather than agreeing with a stale copy of itself.
    escapes = compiler_escapes(ESCAPE_SOURCE)
    assert escapes.len() >= LEAST_ESCAPES

    # All six of § 2's escapes, not the three letters. Half of them were outside
    # this check until 2026-09-14, and the review demonstrated a grammar that
    # drops `\\` and the quotes passing at 20/0 while painting three correct
    # programs as errors.
    assert absent_from(want: ["n", "t", "r", "\\", "\"", "'"], got: escapes).len() == 0
    assert absent_from(want: escapes, got: ["n", "t", "r", "\\", "\"", "'"]).len() == 0

    # The two quotes are gated on context, so the SWAP the review found is
    # visible: a string admits `"` and not `'`, a character literal the reverse.
    assert absent_from(want: for_context(want: escapes, delimiter: "\""), got: ["n", "t", "r", "\\", "\""]).len() == 0
    assert absent_from(want: ["'"], got: for_context(want: escapes, delimiter: "\"")).len() == 1
    assert absent_from(want: ["\""], got: for_context(want: escapes, delimiter: "'")).len() == 1
    reserved = compiler_keywords(KEYWORD_SOURCE)
    assert reserved.len() >= LEAST_KEYWORDS
    assert absent_from(want: ["constant", "function", "record", "variant"], got: reserved).len() == 0

    # The foreign table lives in the same file and must not leak in: `elif` is
    # a word the lexer REFUSES, and painting it as a keyword would show a legal
    # identifier to the author as syntax.
    assert absent_from(want: ["elif"], got: reserved).len() == 1

test "one side missing is reported in the direction it is missing"
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    escapes_agree(@r, want: ["n", "t", "r"], got: ["n", "t"], where: "a string")
    assert r.failed == 1
    assert r.passed == 0
    ok_report: report.Report @ report.empty()
    escapes_agree(@ok_report, want: ["n", "t", "r"], got: ["r", "n", "t"], where: "a string")
    assert ok_report.passed == 1

    # Order is not the claim. A class written `[rnt]` colours the same three
    # escapes, and a check that reddened on it would be reporting the notation.
    assert ok_report.failed == 0

test "the ledger's rows are read, and only the rows"
    figures = ledger_figures(
        "| value | when | what moved it |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2231 | panel 023 | the first |\n| 3512 | panel 084 | the newest |\n"
    )
    assert figures == [2231, 3512]

    # The real record, which is what the check runs against.
    real = read_file(LEDGER)
    assert !real.is_err()
    rows = ledger_figures(real.must())
    assert rows.len() == LEDGER_ROWS
    assert rows[rows.len() - 1] == SPEC_TOKENS

test "the lock fires when the record and the pin disagree"
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(scratch).is_err()

    # A corrupted newest row — the shape panel 086's warden predicts: 7 passed,
    # 1 failed, and the failure prints both numbers.
    figures = ledger_figures("| 2231 | panel 023 | first |\n| 9999 | panel 084 | newest |\n")
    assert figures[figures.len() - 1] != SPEC_TOKENS

    # A dropped row: the count guard, which comparing the newest alone misses.
    assert ledger_figures("| 2231 | panel 023 | only |\n").len() != LEDGER_ROWS
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "the compiler's two lists are read out of its own source"
    reserved = names_in_table(path: BUILTIN_SOURCE, marker: "Builtin(name: ")
    assert reserved.len() >= LEAST_BUILTINS

    # Named rather than merely counted: a table read as the wrong shape can
    # still be long.
    joined = reserved.join(" ")
    assert strings.contains(text: joined, needle: "print")
    assert strings.contains(text: joined, needle: "to_str")
    refused = words_in_arms(path: KEYWORD_SOURCE, marker: "=> ok(ForeignWord(")
    assert refused.len() >= LEAST_FOREIGN
    words = refused.join(" ")
    assert strings.contains(text: words, needle: "fn")
    assert strings.contains(text: words, needle: "struct")

    # An arm carrying three words gives three, which a one-per-line reader
    # would have missed.
    assert strings.contains(text: words, needle: "nil")
    assert strings.contains(text: words, needle: "None")
    assert strings.contains(text: words, needle: "catch")

test "every check here can fire — CLAUDE.md §9, a check nobody has seen fail is a decoration"
    reserved = names_in_table(path: BUILTIN_SOURCE, marker: "Builtin(name: ")
    refused = words_in_arms(path: KEYWORD_SOURCE, marker: "=> ok(ForeignWord(")

    # A document that mentions no built-in at all: every reserved name is
    # missing, and the failure says so by name.
    silent: report.Report @ report.empty()
    named(@silent, spec: "a document with prose and no code spans", reserved: reserved)
    assert silent.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: silent.failures[0], needle: "never mentions them")

    # A document offering a name the compiler does not have. It trips the
    # truncation guard first, which is itself the check: a list that reads as
    # one name is a delimiter that broke.
    invented: report.Report @ report.empty()
    offered(@invented, spec: "Built-ins: `nosuchbuiltin` .", reserved: reserved)
    assert invented.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: invented.failures[0], needle: "truncated")

    # A refused word inside a code span — `fn` is the case that happened.
    foreign: report.Report @ report.empty()
    rejected(@foreign, spec: "the type is `(fn(A) -> B)` here", refused: refused)
    assert foreign.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: foreign.failures[0], needle: "prescribed fix")

    # A letter above ASCII: the pre-tokeniser's premise dying, which is the
    # one thing this inventory exists to catch.
    letter: report.Report @ report.empty()
    inventory(@letter, spec: "a naïve sentence")
    assert letter.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: letter.failures[0], needle: "is it a")

    # And the floors: an extractor that reads the wrong shape reads nothing,
    # and two checks would then pass vacuously.
    empty_list: [str] = []
    starved: report.Report @ report.empty()
    named(@starved, spec: "`print`", reserved: empty_list)
    rejected(@starved, spec: "`print`", refused: empty_list)
    assert starved.failed == 2
    assert strings.contains(text: starved.failures[0], needle: "vacuously")

test "the drift check fires when the two instruments disagree on the sum"
    # A stub compiler again, and for the same reason: what is under test is the
    # comparison, not the real spec, whose size this suite reads on every run.
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(scratch).is_err()
    line = "pre-registered falsifiable prediction (panel 012). Headroom: 131 against the 4096 ceiling"

    # Says a sum, and it is the wrong one: the shape a drifted `measure` has.
    stale = scratch + "/stub-stale.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: stale, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho '" + line + " is 1 and the check goes red at 4096'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", stale]).is_err()
    drifted: report.Report @ report.empty()
    spendable(@drifted, scratch: scratch, compiler: stale)
    assert drifted.failed == 1

    # The message has to name BOTH places, because "make the printed number
    # match" is as likely to be the wrong repair as the right one.
    assert strings.contains(text: drifted.failures[0], needle: "one printed and one checked")

    # And the sum this file computes passes.
    good = scratch + "/stub-good.sh"
    want = "is " + (REAL_TOKENS + FFI_FLOOR).to_str() + " and the check goes red at " + CEILING.to_str()
    owns = "against the contract's own " + CONTRACT_CEILING.to_str()

    # The stub ignores its argument, so it has to satisfy BOTH halves: the
    # check asks the spec's sum and the contract's own ceiling of the same
    # compiler, and a stub that answers one is a stub that fails the other.
    # DOUBLE quotes on the second line and single on the first, which is not
    # taste: the contract's sentence carries an apostrophe, and an apostrophe
    # inside `echo '…'` closes the quote and takes the rest of the line with
    # it. The first version of this stub did exactly that.
    assert !write_file(path: good, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho '" + line + " " + want + "'\necho \"" + owns + "\"\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", good]).is_err()
    agreed: report.Report @ report.empty()
    spendable(@agreed, scratch: scratch, compiler: good)
    assert agreed.failed == 0
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "the record has to be printed by the tool, and still be called the binding number"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(scratch).is_err()
    row = "  real                 " + REAL_TOKENS.to_str() + "   " + REAL_MODEL + ", " + REAL_TAKEN

    # The tool as it stands: the record, then the label. **Both records since
    # 2026-09-12** — the stub ignores its argument, so one script has to answer
    # for the spec AND for the contract, and `recorded` now reads a row for each.
    # **Each row carries its OWN date since 2026-09-13**: the two documents are
    # refreshed independently, and this stub said so a day before the check did.
    contract_row = "  real                 " + CONTRACT_REAL_TOKENS.to_str() + "   " + REAL_MODEL + ", " + CONTRACT_REAL_TAKEN
    speaks = scratch + "/stub-real.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: speaks, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho '" + row + " — the binding number'\necho '" + contract_row + " — the binding number'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", speaks]).is_err()
    agrees: report.Report @ report.empty()
    recorded(@agrees, scratch: scratch, compiler: speaks)
    assert agrees.failed == 0

    # A number this file does not hold. The count that produced this check was
    # 5094 and the repair moved it to 5128, so the drift under test is the one
    # that actually happened.
    older = scratch + "/stub-older.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: older, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho '  real                 5094   " + REAL_MODEL + ", " + REAL_TAKEN + " — the binding number'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", older]).is_err()
    drifted: report.Report @ report.empty()
    recorded(@drifted, scratch: scratch, compiler: older)
    assert drifted.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: drifted.failures[0], needle: "one printed and one checked")

    # And the number right with the LABEL wrong, which is the state this
    # project was in for four milestones: a figure a reader had every reason
    # to trust, attached to OpenAI's tokeniser.
    mislabelled = scratch + "/stub-label.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: mislabelled, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho '" + row + " — a lower bound, not the reader s tokeniser'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", mislabelled]).is_err()
    trusted: report.Report @ report.empty()
    recorded(@trusted, scratch: scratch, compiler: mislabelled)
    assert trusted.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: trusted.failures[0], needle: "cost 998 tokens")
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "the contract's ceiling fires, and a ceiling above it does not"
    # A stub compiler, for the reason the next test gives: what is under test is
    # the comparison and the message, not the real file, whose size the suite
    # itself checks on every run.
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(scratch).is_err()
    fat = scratch + "/stub-fat.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: fat, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 'CLAUDE.md'\necho '  maximum            7000   a lower bound, not the reader s tokeniser'\necho '  real               9999   claude-opus-5, 2026-09-12 — the binding number'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", fat]).is_err()

    over: report.Report @ report.empty()
    contract(@over, scratch: scratch, compiler: fat, ceiling: 5500)
    assert over.failed == 1

    # **AND THE REAL ROW IS THE ONE IT READS, which is panel 134's R5 and the
    # defect that would have made this whole repair a no-op.** The stub above
    # prints BOTH rows and they disagree on purpose: 7000 vendored against 9999
    # real. A check reading `maximum` would compare 7000 to a ceiling of 9000 and
    # pass, forever, on the vendored scale the move exists to abolish — and it
    # would never be noticed, because the gap it hides is exactly the one the
    # contract was already inside. 9999 is over, 7000 is not, so this one
    # assertion separates the two readings.
    reads_real: report.Report @ report.empty()
    contract(@reads_real, scratch: scratch, compiler: fat, ceiling: 9000)
    assert reads_real.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: reads_real.failures[0], needle: "9999")

    # A tool that prints only the vendored row has WITHHELD its verdict, and the
    # check must refuse rather than fall back to the number it can see.
    vendored_only = scratch + "/stub-vendored.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: vendored_only, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 'CLAUDE.md'\necho '  maximum            7000   a lower bound, not the reader s tokeniser'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", vendored_only]).is_err()
    no_real: report.Report @ report.empty()
    contract(@no_real, scratch: scratch, compiler: vendored_only, ceiling: 9000)
    assert no_real.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: no_real.failures[0], needle: "no `real` row")

    # The message has to send the reader to the two places a rule can move to,
    # because "make the number bigger" is the wrong repair and the obvious one.
    assert strings.contains(text: over.failures[0], needle: "docs/records/contract/case-law.md")
    assert strings.contains(text: over.failures[0], needle: ".claude/rules/")

    under: report.Report @ report.empty()
    contract(@under, scratch: scratch, compiler: fat, ceiling: 10000)
    assert under.failed == 0
    assert under.passed == 1

    # And a compiler that cannot answer is a failure rather than a pass, which is
    # the shape a vacuous check would take here.
    mute = scratch + "/stub-silent.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: mute, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 'no tables here'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", mute]).is_err()
    blind: report.Report @ report.empty()
    contract(@blind, scratch: scratch, compiler: mute, ceiling: 10000)
    assert blind.failed == 1
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "the vendored maximum is read off `measure`, whatever the compiler is"
    # POSIX is this case's subject, and this machine answers for itself
    # (can_run_scripts carries the measurement).
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = shell.own_scratch()
    assert !shell.mkdir_all(scratch).is_err()
    path = scratch + "/stub-measure.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: path, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 'spec/heroes-spec.md'\necho '  maximum            9999   any prose at all, because the parser reads the row NAME'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", path]).is_err()
    assert maximum_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: path, path: SPEC).default(-1) == 9999

    mute = scratch + "/stub-mute.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: mute, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 'nothing useful'\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", mute]).is_err()
    assert maximum_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: mute, path: SPEC).is_err()

    # Exit 2 is the tool unable to run, and it must not be read as a number.
    broken = scratch + "/stub-broken.sh"
    assert !write_file(path: broken, text: "#!/bin/sh\necho 'error: no tables' 1>&2\nexit 2\n").is_err()
    assert !shell.run(scratch: scratch, program: "chmod", words: ["chmod", "+x", broken]).is_err()
    assert maximum_of(scratch: scratch, compiler: broken, path: SPEC).is_err()
    assert !shell.remove_tree(scratch).is_err()

test "a quoted word is read, and an unquoted line is refused"
    assert first_quoted(text: "a \"word\" here", from: 0).default("") == "word"
    assert first_quoted(text: "nothing quoted", from: 0).is_err()
    assert first_quoted(text: "\"one\" \"two\"", from: 6).default("") == "two"

    # An unterminated quote is not half a word.
    assert first_quoted(text: "a \"open", from: 0).is_err()

test "the headings are the thirteen sections, numbered from 1 and in order"
    numbered = numbered_sections()
    assert numbered.len() == SECTIONS.len()
    assert numbered[0] == "1. Files and layout"
    assert numbered[12] == "13. FFI"

test "a section citation is judged where it names the spec, by title or by number"
    none: [str] = []
    assert unknown_anchors("as spec § Types says", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("(spec, § FFI; panel 090)", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("the code is spec § Failure).", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("see spec § 7 and spec § 13", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("but spec § 14 is nobody", other: none).len() == 1
    assert unknown_anchors("and spec § Widths is gone", other: none) == ["Widths is gone"]

    # Another document's section is that document's business: CLAUDE.md's,
    # design.md's, the reserved-word registry's.
    assert unknown_anchors("CLAUDE.md § Verification and design.md §4.19", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("(spec § Types; design.md § 4.3)", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("from spec/reserved-words.md § keywords", other: none).len() == 0

    # A section cited right after a markdown file's name is that file's,
    # backticked or bare, however long the name the window cuts; the spec's
    # own file is judged as the spec is. The first two are the shapes lane
    # bounded wrote, which this check read as six citations of the spec.
    assert unknown_anchors("D3a*, `.claude/rules/verification.md` § Bounded discovery): a tag", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("the class rule (.claude/rules/records.md § The lists): every item", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("as `.claude/rules/diagnostics-and-goldens.md` § Golden discipline says", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("see `spec/heroes-spec.md` § Widths", other: none) == ["Widths"]
    assert unknown_anchors("see `spec/heroes-spec.md` § 13 and \"heroes-spec.md\" § Types", other: none).len() == 0

    # A bare `§ Title` with no document named is judged: a real section holds,
    # a made-up one is caught, and another document's heading passes through
    # `other`, which `anchors` reads from those documents.
    assert unknown_anchors("syntax (§ Functions and calls)", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("the rule (§ Widths) states", other: none).len() == 1
    assert unknown_anchors("# § Commands", other: ["Commands"]).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("# § Commands", other: none).len() == 1

    # `spec` is a word: `inspect` and `respect` are not it (panel 126's
    # compiler seat, with a probe), and a dotted number is design.md's.
    assert unknown_anchors("inspect § Verification first", other: ["Verification"]).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("respect § 14 of the contract", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("spec § 4.19 says", other: none).len() == 1

    # A title cut by a line wrap is still the title; two letters are not.
    assert unknown_anchors("(§4.19, spec § Strings,", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("is `@` (spec § Functions and", other: none).len() == 0
    assert unknown_anchors("see spec § Fu", other: none).len() == 1
